# Chapter 4: Format.  The chapter is kept in several sources that this file
# reads: how formats combine and what the screen shows (format1, format2),
# the six entries of the Global menu in the order of the menu (format3 to
# format7) and the local formats and the messages (format8).
.doc Format
.chapter 4
.h1 Format [format]
.ix Format
.ix Formats
.ix Format settings
.ix Global menu
.ix Global, Format
.ix Global format
.ix Local format
.ix Panels of the Global menu
A format is a group of settings that says how text looks and how a
document is laid out: the attributes of the characters, the justification
and the indents of the lines, the tab stops, the spacing between blocks and
sections, the typefaces, the page margins and the way the markers are
shown.  This chapter describes where each setting is made, what values it
can have, what its default is and what it does on the screen and on the
printed page.

The settings come in two kinds.  The global settings are made on the Global
menu of the {k:F12} bar and hold for the whole document until something more
local overrides them.  The local settings are made with {k:F6} (or
{k:Ctrl-E}) and with the attribute keys and belong to a piece of text, a
column or a block.

The chapter follows the menus, because a setting is found by asking where it
is made.  First come the rules that hold for every setting: how global and
local settings combine, and what the edit screen shows of them.  Then come the
six entries of the Global menu, one section each, in the order of the menu.
Each section describes the panels of its entry field by field.  A subject such
as indents, tab stops or section tags is explained once, with the panel on
which it is changed.  The local formats, which are made with keys and not with
panels, and the messages of the panels come last.
.table 19 41
Section | What it covers
{x:format-kinds} | Global and local settings, their order, the defaults.
{x:format-screen} | What the edit screen draws of a format.
{x:format-global-menu} | The Global menu and the keys of its panels.
{x:format-format} | Attributes, justification, indents, tabs, spacing,
 section tags and fonts of headlines and text.
{x:format-page} | Paper, margins, page numbers and the title page.
{x:format-typeface} | The names of the twenty fonts.
{x:format-marker} | Markers: display, dates, counters, notes, keycaps.
{x:format-options} | The settings of the Editor and the backup file.
{x:format-named} | Named formats for blocks.
{x:format-local} | Formats of a block, a column or characters.
{x:format-messages} | The messages of the panels.
.end

